<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kashmiri Pandits</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kashmiri Pandits are the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> community of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> valley, long prominent as scholars, scribes and administrators. They served successive rulers, from the Hindu kings to the sultans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogras</a>. Most left the valley in 1990 after threats and killings during the insurgency.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Kashmiri Pandits are part of the larger Saraswat Brahmin community and belong to the Pancha Gauda Brahmin group. They are the only remaining indigenous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> population of the Kashmir Valley after the large-scale conversion of its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Muslim rule was established in the valley only in the fourteenth century, less through invasion than through the weak rule of the Hindu Lohara dynasty, whose last king, Sūhadeva, included the Brahmins in his heavy taxation, though they appear to have been exempt before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sultan Sikandar Butshikan (1389–1413) destroyed many non-Muslim religious symbols and forced people to convert or flee, and some Pandits left for other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, perhaps also drawn by grants of land from rulers who valued their learning. His heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zain-ul-abidin">Zain-ul-Abidin</a> (1423–74) allowed those forcibly converted to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>, helped restore temples, gave the Pandits land and encouraged those who had left to come back. The community was favoured under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rule (1846–1947) and made up about 6 per cent of the valley's population in 1947, but 20 per cent of the Pandits left after the land reforms of 1950.</p><p>Far larger numbers left in the 1990s, during the eruption of militancy, after persecution and threats by radical Islamists and militants. On 19 January 1990 mosques issued declarations that Pandit men had to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> or be killed. According to a number of authors about 100,000 of some 140,000 Pandits left the valley; others give higher figures. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and Kashmir government report counted 219 members of the community killed in the region between 1989 and 2004. Many refugees lived in camps in Jammu, and by 2011 only an estimated 2,700-3,400 Pandits remained in the valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The community is divided into three subcastes, which probably took shape in the 14th century: the Guru or Bāchabat, priests; the Jotish, astrologers; and the Kārkun, historically employed mainly by the government, to which most belong. All three dine and teach together, but only the Jotish and Kārkun intermarry. Major festivals are Shivratri, called Herath in Kashmiri, and Navreh, the lunar new year, and the Mata Kheerbhawani shrine at Tullamulla is among the holiest. Pandits traditionally eat mutton and fish but not beef or pork. Descent is reckoned in the male line, with family names such as Kaul, Dhar, Raina and Sapru.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kashmiri Pandits” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan Sikandar is remembered for destroying temples, while Zain al-Abidin, known as Budshah, the great king, restored tolerance, recalled Hindu <b>Pandits</b>, had Sanskrit works translated into Persian and built canals and bridges.</blockquote><a href="/state/kashmir-sultanate">Kashmir Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
